Lol5nPXKwXRi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 90:82a7616e63686f727391da003d2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f7475692f6f7665726c61792e676f3a313a312d3431333a31ac616e737765724c656e677468d1091f This file implements a terminal status-bar overlay for a child process running under a PTY. Overlay wraps an exec.Cmd started via pty.Start (NewOverlay), holding the pty master, the real stdout, and mutex-guarded layout state (height, visible, swRow, cols) plus a bubbletea Program for rendering the bar. Run puts stdin into raw mode, installs a SIGWINCH handler that resizes the PTY and re-establishes the scroll region (and self-sends one SIGWINCH to initialize), then bridges I/O: stdin -> ptmx in a goroutine, and ptmx -> a lockedWriter around stdout in the foreground; when the child's output ends it stops the bar, restores the terminal, and returns cmd.Wait(). The overlay reserves the bottom rows of the terminal: setScrollRegion emits DECSTBM to confine the child to the top rows, draws a '─' divider line when reserved >= 2, records the status-bar row (swRow) and width, and notifies the bubbletea program of the new width. resizePty shrinks the child's PTY by the reserved rows so the child never draws over the bar. Show/Hide/SetHeight toggle visibility/height and call applyLayout, which clears the affected bottom rows, resets the scroll region, and resizes the PTY (SetHeight clears max(prev,new) rows to erase leftovers). SetStatus/SetSubStatus store sections and forward them to the bubbletea program as statusMsg/subStatusMsg. The bar itself is barModel, a bubbletea model: Init starts an 80ms shimmer tick; Update handles status/sub/width messages and advances a shimmer phase; View renders the status left-aligned and the sub-status right-aligned (padding with spaces, leaving the last column free to avoid wrapping) and applies a shimmer effect plus ANSI reset. startProgram runs the program with a pipe as input (no real keyboard input), no signal handler, and a regionWriter as output; stop quits the program, waits for its goroutine via the done channel, and resets the scroll region. Two writer adapters serialize terminal writes: lockedWriter just wraps stdout writes in the overlay mutex (used for child output); regionWriter strips hide/show-cursor sequences from bubbletea frames, drops the frame entirely if the bar is hidden or height < 2, and otherwise wraps it in save-cursor / move-to-swRow / clear-line / restore-cursor so the bar repaints in its reserved row without disturbing the child's cursor.
aCzaUrsD71Ji5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 92:82a7616e63686f727391da003f2f55736572732f776f726b73706163652f576f726b73706163652f636170746e2f706b672f7475692f6f7665726c61792e676f3a3130303a312d3130383a32ac616e737765724c656e677468d10261 SetSubStatus is a thread-safe setter for the overlay's sub-status sections. Under the mutex it stores the variadic sections into o.sub and loads the current bubbletea program pointer into a local; it then releases the lock before conditionally sending the sections to the program as a subStatusMsg. Storing sections in the struct means a program started later can pick them up at initialization, while the Send delivers a live update to an already-running program; copying prog to a local and sending outside the lock avoids holding the mutex during Send and races with concurrent start/stop that swap o.prog.
eUXTyy8hQExi5N0YAKMbRAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA 156: This is a load dependency: the referencing code reads shared Overlay state rather than mutating it independently. Concretely, SetSubStatus loads the o.prog field (the running bubbletea *tea.Program) while holding o.mu, capturing it into a local variable before unlocking. That loaded value is then used outside the critical section to deliver the subStatusMsg via prog.Send if the program exists. Loading under the mutex guarantees a consistent snapshot of prog against concurrent startProgram/stop calls that assign or nil it out, and the nil check handles the case where the bar program is not currently running.
